  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 305 AM MST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nOrganized ridging will attempt to build back in over the area on \nTuesday into Wednesday behind Monday's weak system. The split trough \nwill mostly move out Tuesday morning, but the northern upper level \nlow will be diving southward simultaneously. This will help drop \nsurface pressure over northeast Wyoming or South Dakota, building \nthe cross-barrier pressure gradient once again. Expect breezy \nwesterlies to return to areas along and west of the Laramie Range \nTuesday, and peak in strength Tuesday night with the maximum \npressure gradient before the surface low begins to eject early \nWednesday. Low-level height gradients are a little lower than usual \nthresholds for high winds per most model forecasts, but MSLP \ngradients are pretty strong. This should limit winds to just the gap \nwind areas. Current probabilities are around 60% for Arlington/Elk \nMountain and 30% for Bordeaux and the I-80 summit. Tuesday's highs \nshould be around 5F warmer than Monday with more sunshine returning, \nand then Wednesday's highs look similar, with the warming trend \nhampered by a reinforcing chilly airmass moving in behind the \nnorthern branch shortwave. Dewpoints will also be dropping during \nthis period, so elevated fire weather conditions may return again, \nprimarily in Albany, Laramie, and Platte counties. \n\nThe gradual warming trend will continue Thanksgiving Day through the \nweekend. A weak vort-max will pass through around Wednesday \nnight/Thursday, but nearly all ensemble members are now dry for this \nevent, and it will largely just slow the warming trend. Another \nround of stronger ridging will build into the west Friday into \nSaturday. the ridge will have a fairly potent jet streak aloft on \nits northeast flank corresponding to a temperature gradient. Exactly \nwhere this sets up will determine out weather outcome for Friday \ninto Saturday. Ensembles are clustered into two scenarios. The \nfirst, made of of nearly all GEFS members and about 40% of ECMWF \nensemble members includes a stronger, more amplified ridge that \npushes the jet streak just to our northeast. This would lead to a \nsignificant warm-up Friday into the weekend. While conditions would \nstill be breezy to windy, this scenario pushes the strongest \ngradients just far enough away to reduce the chance for high winds. \nIn the other scenario, made up of the remaining 60% ECMWF ensemble \nmembers, the ridge is weaker and flatter, positioning the jet streak \nmore or less directly over the area. This would lead to stronger \nwinds (likely high winds), and the potential for respectable \nmountain snowfall. In addition, this scenario would mean cooler \ntemperatures, and the potential for a stalled (weak) arctic front \nsomewhere nearby. For now, the official forecast is closer to the \nfirst scenario, which has roughly a 60% chance of playing out, but \nelevated wind speeds and low PoPs for mountain snowfall are still \nincluded. For now, it looks fairly likely that Cheyenne will add\nat least a week to the snow-less streak after surpassing the\nrecord latest first measurable snowfall of the season on \nFriday.",
